{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"66a21c2b8216e02faf249bc93bdfdd31df63fbb61cc59639f95df8792d0a1d12","slug":"rational-functions-and-partial-fractions","location":"/_pages/3.4_Rational_functions_and_partial_fractions.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Rational Functions and Partial Fractions","content_includes_title":false,"github":"https://github.com/katiesteckles/MR-discrete-book","numbering":{"title":{"enabled":true,"offset":1},"code":{"enabled":false},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":false},"heading_1":{"enabled":true},"heading_2":{"enabled":true},"heading_3":{"enabled":true},"heading_4":{"enabled":true},"heading_5":{"enabled":true},"heading_6":{"enabled":true}},"source_url":"https://github.com/katiesteckles/MR-discrete-book/blob/main/_pages/3.4_Rational_functions_and_partial_fractions.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/katiesteckles/MR-discrete-book/edit/main/_pages/3.4_Rational_functions_and_partial_fractions.md","enumerator":"3.4","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"3.4_Rational_functions_and_partial_fractions.md","url":"/3-366ce9a3936977a563ed7e1091718219.4_Rational_functions_and_partial_fractions.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.2 Rational Functions and their Partial Fraction Expansion:\nRational functions are those functions that are quotients of polynomial functions. So a\nrational function f(x), is a real-valued function of the form\nP\n(\nx\n)\n(\nf=\nx\n)\nQ\n(\nx\n)\nwhere both P(x) and Q(x) are polynomials, and the degree of P(x) is smaller than the\ndegree of Q(x). Recall that the degree of a polynomial is the largest exponent in the\npolynomial. So, let’s do a quick review of partial fractions.\nPartial fractions can only be done if the degree of the numerator is strictly less than the\ndegree of the denominator. That is important to remember. So, once